<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah U'Betzi'as HaPas 3</div>

<div class="lm-subtitle">
  Based on the discourse <span class="verse">"Vayehi mikeitz — ki merachamam yenahaigem"</span>
  <span class="source-ref">(<span class="lm-ref">Likutay Moharan II, 7</span>)</span> — see there. Concerning what is written there, that through the inclusiveness of son and student, one merits the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> [Divine Will] at the time of eating, etc.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 1                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 1</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span> for a meal — so that the eating should be in the aspect of holy eating, in which the <span class="key-concept">illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span></span> <span class="heb-def">[Divine Will]</span> is revealed at the time of eating. This is accomplished through the livelihood being drawn from the aspect of the <span class="key-concept">hands that are in the Sea of Wisdom</span> [<span class="heb-term">yam hachochmah</span>], etc. And therefore one merits the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> at the time of eating specifically — see there in the discourse mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span> before eating. For through washing the hands and purifying them with water — through this, one draws upon them the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> from the Sea of Wisdom. And then the hands are included within the aspect of the waters of the Sea. For a <span class="heb-term">revi'is</span> of water is the aspect of a <span class="heb-term">mikveh</span>, as is brought in the <span class="heb-term">poskim</span> <span class="heb-def">[halachic authorities]</span> — that by Torah law, a <span class="heb-term">revi'is</span> purifies [like] a <span class="heb-term">mikveh</span>. And a <span class="heb-term">mikveh</span> is the aspect of a sea, as it is written: <span class="verse">"And the gathering of the waters He called 'seas'"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Genesis 1:10)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, through the <span class="heb-term">revi'is</span> of water with which one purifies the hands — through this, the hands are included within the aspect of the Sea, that is, within the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom. And then, the primary eating and livelihood are drawn from these hands — for the primary livelihood is drawn from the hands that are in the Sea of Wisdom, as mentioned above. And then, when one merits to receive one's eating from there, then the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> is drawn and revealed at the time of eating — and this is the primary holiness of eating.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the primary purpose — to merit the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> mentioned above. For the Sea of Wisdom mentioned above — where the <span class="heb-term">makifin</span> <span class="heb-def">[surrounding lights]</span> shine, which are the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="key-concept">son</span> — and the <span class="key-concept">student</span> — this is the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> of Moshe Rabbainu, peace be upon him, who illuminated this <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, as is understood there in the discourse mentioned above. And the primary [purpose of] the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> is to save and guard and extricate Israel from transgressions, G-d forbid — for this is the primary compassion toward Israel, the holy nation: for one who knows from where Israel is drawn, [understands] how they are utterly distant from sins, may the Merciful One save us, etc. — see there.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And one who merits to illuminate the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> in Israel and to guard them and save them from every sin and transgression — through this, the <span class="heb-term">makifin</span> mentioned above shine, the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom, from where livelihood is drawn from the hands that are in the Sea mentioned above. And through this, one merits the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> at the time of eating. It emerges that the primary illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> is drawn through <span class="key-concept">being guarded from transgressions</span> — which is drawn through the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> mentioned above, which is the aspect of Moshe, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And all the transgressions, G-d forbid — the primary damage they cause is in the aspect of the <span class="key-concept">hands</span>, as is understood in the words of <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, elsewhere. As our Sages, of blessed memory, said: <span class="maxim">"And you will not come to the hands of a transgression"</span> — <span class="verse">"to the <b>hands</b>"</span> of a transgression, specifically. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">"the sadness of the hands"</span> [<span class="heb-term">itzavon yadainu</span>] that is said regarding the generation of the Flood, who rebelled against the Omnipresent. As it is written: <span class="verse">"This one shall comfort us from our work and from the sadness of our hands"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Genesis 5:29)</span>, etc. For the primary blemish — which is the aspect of sadness, the aspect of <span class="verse">"and He was saddened in His heart"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Genesis 6:6)</span> — affects the hands specifically, as is understood in the words of <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span> on the verse "and from the sadness of our hands" mentioned above, which is brought in the discourse <span class="verse">"And on the day of the firstfruits"</span> (<span class="lm-ref">Likutay Moharan I, 56</span>) — see there.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For the primary blemish of all the transgressions affects the hands — which are the aspect of the totality of the entire Torah, the totality of the entire world. As Rabbi Elazar the Great said before his passing — he raised his two hands and said: <span class="maxim">"Woe for the two Torahs that are lost from the world"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Sanhedrin 68a)</span>. And as it is written: <span class="verse">"My hand also laid the foundation of the earth, and My right hand spread out the heavens"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 48:13)</span>. For the Holy One, blessed be He, created the world through the Torah — which is the aspect of the two hands. And therefore, the primary blemish, G-d forbid, reaches the hands. And then the hands are in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"And the hands of Moshe were heavy"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Exodus 17:12)</span>. For Moshe is the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> that shines in Israel to guard them and save them from the hands of transgression. And one into whom a <span class="heb-term">ru'ach shtus</span> <span class="heb-def">[spirit of folly]</span> enters and who removes the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> from himself, G-d forbid, and commits some sin and transgression — then the blemish reaches Moshe, who is the totality of the Torah. And the primary blemish is in the hands, as mentioned above, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"And the <b>hands</b> of Moshe were heavy."</span> The <b>hands</b> specifically, as mentioned above. And this is what is said: <span class="verse">"And he guards his hand from doing any evil"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 56:2)</span>. "Guards his <b>hand</b>" — specifically.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 2                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 2</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  It emerges that the primary blemish is in the hands. And therefore, one needs to raise the hands toward the mind [<span class="heb-term">mo'ach</span>], which is the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Lift up your hands in holiness and bless"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 134:2)</span>, etc. — in order to draw the illumination of the holiness of the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, which is the aspect of Moshe, upon the hands specifically — in order to be saved through this from the hands of transgression. For all sins depend on the hands specifically, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span> — to purify the hands specifically, through the waters of <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, which are the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom mentioned above. For this <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> saves and purifies Israel from all transgressions, as mentioned above. And this is done before eating specifically — so that the eating should be in the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 3                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 3</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And one who is dismissive, G-d forbid, of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span> and eats without washing — this is the aspect of the blemish of <span class="key-concept">eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil</span>, which encompasses all the transgressions in the Torah. For the primary blemish of eating from the Tree of Knowledge is in the aspect of the hands, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"And she took of its fruit and ate"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Genesis 3:6)</span>, etc.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And the primary blemish was that they ate food in which the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> does not shine. For the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> is above everything — even above the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> of holiness. And there, in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, the <span class="heb-term">sitra achra</span> has no hold whatsoever. For <span class="maxim">"everything was refined in thought"</span> [<span class="heb-term">kula b'machshavah isbariru</span>] <span class="source-ref">(Zohar)</span>. But above thought, above <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, which is above everything — they have no hold whatsoever.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, Hashem, blessed be He, desired that Adam HaRishon eat only eating that is entirely in the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>. But below the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> — where there is some hold for the aspect of the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil — from there he should not eat at all. For the primary purpose of the creation of man was for the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> — that he should merit in completeness the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, which is the ultimate completeness, as is understood in the words of <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, in many places. And in particular in the stories that he told us, some of which have already been printed in <span class="heb-term">Sichos HaRa"n</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And Moshe Rabbainu, the master of all the prophets — the ultimate completeness he reached at the time of his passing was that he reached the ultimate completeness of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, for he passed away within the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ra'ava d'ra'avin</span> <span class="heb-def">[the Will of Wills]</span>, the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> that is within the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>. And Adam HaRishon was created so that he should immediately reach this level — in completeness — to the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>. And therefore, He immediately placed him in <span class="heb-term">Gan Eden</span> and commanded him: <span class="verse">"From every tree of the garden you shall surely eat"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Genesis 2:16)</span>, etc.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And at first glance, this is difficult: Why was he <em>commanded</em> to eat from every tree of the garden? After all, the primary warning was that he should not eat from the Tree of Knowledge! And certainly regarding the other trees, about which he was not warned — who would prevent him from eating from them? And why was it said to him explicitly: "From every tree of the garden you shall surely eat"?!
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  Rather, eating the fruits of the trees of <span class="heb-term">Gan Eden</span> is itself the primary <span class="key-concept">completeness of man</span>. For they are holy eating mentioned above, the aspect of eating in which the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> shines. For through such eating, one attains ultimate completeness. And this is the aspect of <span class="key-concept">eating on Shabbos</span>, the aspect of eating the <span class="heb-term">man</span> [manna]. For the manna that Israel ate is the aspect of eating that is entirely in the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> — the aspect of <span class="heb-term">mah</span> <span class="heb-def">[What?]</span>, the aspect of Moshe. As is explained in the discourse mentioned above. For it was called <span class="heb-term">man</span> [manna] because "they did not know what [<span class="heb-term">mah</span>] it was" <span class="source-ref">(Exodus 16:15)</span> — for the manna descended in the merit of Moshe, who is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">mah</span>, the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of <span class="key-concept">eating on Shabbos</span>, which is the aspect of eating the manna. As it is written: <span class="verse">"And Moshe said: 'Eat it today'"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Exodus 16:25)</span> — which was said regarding the eating of the manna. And from there we derive the primary [obligation of] eating on Shabbos, as our Sages, of blessed memory, derived from this verse to eat <span class="key-concept">three meals on Shabbos</span>. For eating on Shabbos is the aspect of manna, as is brought — that for this reason we place the bread between two coverings, as a remembrance of the manna, etc. For eating on Shabbos is entirely the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>. For Shabbos is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, the aspect of <span class="verse">"with love and with <span class="heb-term">ratzon</span> You bequeathed to us…"</span> [from the Shabbos prayers], the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ra'ava d'ra'avin</span> [Will of Wills] that is revealed on Shabbos.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For eating on Shabbos is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">oneg Shabbos</span> [the delight of Shabbos], the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Then you shall delight <b>upon</b> Hashem"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 58:14)</span>. "<b>Upon</b> Hashem" — this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, as is known. For <span class="heb-term">oneg</span> [<span style="font-weight:bold;">ענ״ג</span>] stands for: <span style="font-weight:bold;">ע</span>דן [Eden], <span style="font-weight:bold;">נ</span>הר [River], <span style="font-weight:bold;">ג</span>ן [Garden]. As it is found in the Holy Zohar, the aspect of: <span class="verse">"And a river goes out of Eden to water the garden"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Genesis 2:10)</span>. And <span class="heb-term">Eden</span> is the aspect of <span class="verse">"no eye has seen"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 64:3)</span>, etc. — which is the aspect of the comprehension of the Master [<span class="heb-term">hasagas haRav</span>], which is the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, as is understood in the discourse mentioned above. And "a river goes out of Eden" — this is the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom mentioned above, which is drawn from the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> through the aspect of Moshe, as mentioned above — which waters the garden, meaning the plants and grasses that grow in the garden — that is, the souls of Israel, to water them and give them life, to draw upon them holiness and purity.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-concept">three meals of Shabbos</span>, which are the aspect of <span class="heb-term">oneg Shabbos</span> — Eden, River, Garden — as is brought in the Holy Zohar. This is the aspect of the illumination of son and student, which is the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom mentioned above, which is the aspect of the river that goes out of Eden, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For the meal of <span class="key-concept">Friday night</span> is the aspect of the illumination of the student — which is the aspect of <span class="verse">"the fullness of the whole earth is His glory"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 6:3)</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span>, <span class="heb-term">chakal tapuchin kadishin</span> <span class="heb-def">[the field of holy apples]</span> <span class="source-ref">(Zohar)</span>. The <span class="key-concept">morning meal</span> is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Atika Kadisha</span> <span class="heb-def">[the Holy Ancient One]</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Keser</span> <span class="heb-def">[Crown]</span>, as is known, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ayeh</span> <span class="heb-def">[Where?]</span> — as we say then in <span class="heb-term">Mussaf</span>: <span class="verse">"Where is the place of His glory?"</span> etc. And this is the aspect of the comprehension of the son, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Ayeh</span>. <span class="key-concept"><span class="heb-term">Shalosh Seudos</span></span> <span class="heb-def">[the Third Meal]</span> is the aspect of the inclusiveness of son and student — which is the primary completeness, that they should be included in one another. For the Third Meal is the aspect of the meal of <span class="heb-term">Zeir Anpin</span>, the aspect of the inheritance of Yaakov, who <span class="verse">"traverses from one end to the other"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Shabbos 118b)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  It emerges that eating on Shabbos is the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the eating of the trees of <span class="heb-term">Gan Eden</span> that Adam HaRishon was commanded regarding: <span class="verse">"From every tree of the garden you shall surely eat."</span> For the eating of the fruit of <span class="heb-term">Gan Eden</span> is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">oneg</span> — for they grow by the river that goes out of Eden, etc., as mentioned above. But from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil, He warned him not to eat at all. For Hashem, blessed be He, desired that his eating should only be from the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, where the <span class="heb-term">sitra achra</span> has no hold whatsoever, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And through Adam HaRishon's eating from the Tree of Knowledge, through this the <span class="heb-term">sitra d'misavah</span> <span class="heb-def">[the side of impurity]</span> was drawn down. And all purifications are through the waters of a <span class="heb-term">mikveh</span>, which are the aspect of "a river goes out of Eden," etc. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span> before eating — to purify the hands from the blemish of the <span class="heb-term">zuhamas hanachash</span> <span class="heb-def">[the contamination of the serpent]</span>, for the primary blemish affects the hands, as mentioned above. And through the water, they are purified and the hands are included in the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Lift up your hands in holiness and bless"</span> — that the hands are included in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Kodesh</span> [Holiness], which is the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom, which is called <span class="heb-term">Kodesh</span>. And then, the eating is drawn from the hands that are in the Sea of Wisdom — and in that eating, the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> is revealed, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For all our eating needs to be in the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, as mentioned above. For even when the food is placed before a person, he still needs to request that the food be brought forth <span class="heb-term">mibei malka</span> <span class="heb-def">[from the King's house]</span>. As it is found in the Holy Zohar regarding <span class="heb-term">Rav Hamnuna Sava</span>, who, even though the food was prepared before him, did not want to begin eating until he prayed for his food. And he said: <span class="maxim">"Until it is given from the King's house"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Zohar I, 88a)</span>, etc. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span> — that we wash the hands so that the food should be drawn <span class="heb-term">mibei malka</span>, from the aspect of the hands that are in the Sea of Wisdom, as mentioned above — so that the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> should be revealed in this food. And only then is it permitted for the eating of Israel, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 4                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 4</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of: <span class="maxim">"The table atones like the Altar"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Berachos 55a)</span>. For through holy eating, the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> is revealed — which is drawn from the aspect of the inclusiveness of son and student, which is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> of Moshe, which is the opposite of transgressions, as mentioned above. This is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">s'lach na</span> <span class="heb-def">[forgive, please]</span> that we merit on <span class="heb-term">Yom Kippur</span> — through which we merit the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Chanukah</span>, the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">chanukas habayis</span> <span class="heb-def">[inauguration of the House]</span>, which is the aspect of the inclusiveness of son and student, as all this is explained in the discourse mentioned above — see there well. It emerges that the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span> that we merit at the time of eating is drawn from the aspect of the forgiveness of sins. And therefore, <span class="maxim">"the table atones like the Altar."</span> For regarding all the sacrifices, it is said <span class="heb-term">l'ratzon</span> <span class="heb-def">[for favor / for <span class="heb-term">ratzon</span>]</span> — the aspect of the illumination of the <span class="heb-term">Ratzon</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"You shall not eat and you shall not touch it, lest you die"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Genesis 3:3)</span> — [Chava] added to the command. For the primary prohibition was the eating.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 5                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 5</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, one who is dismissive of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span> is liable to death — the aspect of the blemish of the Tree of Knowledge, etc. And therefore he becomes poor — for he has no livelihood. For the primary livelihood and wealth are drawn from the <span class="key-concept">hands that are in the Sea of Wisdom</span>, as mentioned above — which is drawn through <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, <span class="heb-term">Rav Chisda</span> said: <span class="maxim">"I washed with handfuls of water, and they gave me handfuls of bounty"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Shabbos 62b)</span>. For from there, wealth and livelihood are drawn — from the aspect of the hands that are in the Sea of Wisdom, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 6                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 6</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore the vessel must be <span class="key-concept">complete</span>. The vessel — this is the aspect of the student, as is brought in the words of <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory (<span class="lm-ref">Likutay Moharan I, 53</span>). And therefore the vessel needs to be complete — so that it should not be lacking, so that it should not let out the water that is poured into it, but rather should be complete and full. And this is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">kos shel berachah</span> <span class="heb-def">[cup of blessing]</span>, which needs to be complete and full. For the illumination of the student is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">melo</span> [fullness], the aspect of: <span class="verse">"the fullness of the whole earth is His glory"</span>; the aspect of: <span class="verse">"And Yehoshua bin Nun was <b>full</b> of the spirit of wisdom, for Moshe had laid his hands upon him"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Deuteronomy 34:9)</span>. "<b>Full</b>" — specifically, as is understood at the end of the discourse mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And then specifically — when the vessel, which is the aspect of the student, is in its completeness and fullness — then it can receive the aspect of the waters of <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, the aspect of the Sea of Wisdom, the aspect of <span class="verse">"the fullness of the whole earth is His glory."</span> And then son and student are included together. And then, through this, one can purify the hands from the aspect of "busy hands," from the aspect of "the sadness of the hands," from the aspect of the hands of transgression, as mentioned above. For this is the main thing — that the students should make other students, as is understood in the words of <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory — meaning, to illuminate the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> in the world, to save them from transgressions. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">netilas yadayim</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- Translator's Summary -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <div class="summary-heading">✦ Translator's Summary ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    This remarkable halacha, based on <strong>Likutay Moharan II, 7</strong>, reveals hand-washing as an act that draws eating from a level <em>above</em> da'as — the level of <em>Ratzon</em> (Divine Will).
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>The "Hands in the Sea of Wisdom":</strong> Washing with a <em>revi'is</em> of water includes the hands in the "Sea of Wisdom" (<em>yam hachochmah</em>). Livelihood then flows not from worldly sources but from these mystical hands — and in that food, the <em>Ratzon</em> is revealed.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>Adam's eating:</strong> "From every tree of the garden you shall surely eat" was not merely permission — it was a <em>command</em>. The fruits of Gan Eden are holy eating, the illumination of the <em>Ratzon</em>. The Tree of Knowledge was below the <em>Ratzon</em>, where the <em>sitra achra</em> has a hold. Adam was meant to eat only from the level above thought — the level of <em>ra'ava d'ra'avin</em> (Will of Wills).
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>The Three Shabbos Meals:</strong> Eating on Shabbos is the aspect of the manna and the fruits of Gan Eden. The word <em>oneg</em> (delight) = <em>Eden</em>, <em>Nahar</em> (River), <em>Gan</em> (Garden). Friday night = the student's illumination (<em>Malchus</em>); Shabbos morning = the son's comprehension (<em>Keser</em> / <em>Ayeh</em>); Shalosh Seudos = the inclusiveness of both — the ultimate completeness.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>The vessel:</strong> Must be complete — corresponding to the student who is "full of the spirit of wisdom" (Yehoshua bin Nun). A broken vessel = broken <em>emunah</em>; a complete vessel = the purified <em>medameh</em> (imagination) that can receive the waters of <em>da'as</em>.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-top:1rem; font-style:italic; text-align:center; color:var(--accent-muted);">
    Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah 4 continues in a separate file.
  </p>
</div>


